From: mixelpixx <11727006+mixelpixx@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] loader: PS4 CPU: FMA gives the fused result bit for bit, in every MXCSR mode

The default FMA replacement computed single precision as a multiply and
an add in double precision, rounded twice, and double precision as a
multiply and an add, each rounded. Results differed from the PS5's in
the last bit (in double precision often, where the sum cancels), and in
some NaN cases. It now gives the fused result bit for bit:

- Single precision: the product is exact in double precision. The sum
  is rounded to odd (TwoSum gives its exact error; where that is nonzero
  and the sum even, the sum moves one ulp toward the exact one) and then
  to float: one correct rounding (Boldo and Melquiond, 2008), in every
  rounding mode, with FTZ and DAZ too.
- Double precision: Dekker's product gives a*b = ph + pl exactly, then
  (th, tl) = TwoSum(ph, c), v = tl + pl rounded to odd, result = th + v,
  all in vector registers. That holds where MXCSR rounds to nearest and
  no intermediate value overflows or is subnormal. A vector test sends
  the other lanes to FmaFallback, a C++ function the code calls with
  every register saved: it adds the integer significands and rounds as
  MXCSR says (rounding control, FTZ after rounding, DAZ).
- NaNs: a's NaN, else b's, else c's (also where a*b is 0 times
  infinity), quieted, with the sign the instruction keeps. An infinite c
  with finite a and b gives c, also where a*b overflows in double
  precision.

The MXCSR status flags are not kept exact. KYTY_JAGUAR_FMA=fast is
unchanged and stays three to four times cheaper.
---
